  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and more humid conditions return this weekend \nwith at least periodic chances for some showers and scattered \nthunderstorms Saturday afternoon through Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure will move from the Ohio Valley to the \nNortheast through tonight. Clearing skies and calm winds will likely \nresult in patchy to areas of fog early this morning. Most of the fog \nwill be confined to valley locations inland from Lake Erie and Lake \nOntario. Fog will erode shortly after daybreak this morning, and dry \nand calm weather will continue across the forecast area through \ntonight. \n\nIncreasing southerly flow on the backside of the departing surface \nhigh will bring warmer and more humid weather into the forecast area \nSaturday. Dewpoint temperatures are expected to rise into the mid to \nupper 60s across western NY by Saturday afternoon. The upper level \npattern suggests that a shortwave trough may move from the Tennessee \nValley to the Northeast. The combination a warm and moist airmass \nahead of an advancing trough will support increasing chances for \nshowers and scattered thunderstorms Saturday afternoon. Ensemble \nguidance suggests that the highest probability of rain will be from \nthe western Southern Tier to the eastern Lake Ontario region. Heavy \ndownpours are possible in any thunderstorms. \n\nA cold front associated with low pressure near Hudson Bay is \nexpected to move across the forecast area late Saturday night \nthrough Sunday. The plume of deep moisture will be exiting to the \neast, and chances of showers and a few storms are higher across the \neastern lake Ontario region Sunday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief period of cool and dry weather is expected \non Monday, before unsettled weather arrives mid-week.\n\nAn upper level trough will dig across the Northeast Monday, with \nsurface high pressure building across the area. A cool, northwest \nflow will move into the forecast area with mean 850mb temperatures \nfalling to around +7C by Monday afternoon. Because the airmass is \nvery dry, little lake response is expected. High temperatures are \nexpected to reach the upper 60s to low 70s. \n\nAn upper level ridge will briefly build across the Great Lakes \nregion Monday night before flattening out through mid-week. Warmer \nair will move back into the region Tuesday with highs in the upper \n70s to low 80s. A series of frontal boundaries may move across the \nregion and bring periods of showers and thunderstorms. Uncertainty \nis high in timing of precipitation.",
